.\" zvolcarve(1) — manual page, mdoc. Kept true to the program by
.\" tests/man-check.sh.
.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt ZVOLCARVE 1
.Os zvolrescue 0.9.12
.Sh NAME
.Nm zvolcarve
.Nd find and extract volumes that no uberblock of a ZFS pool points at any more
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar global options
.Cm scan
.Fl o Ar dir
.Op Ar options
.Op Ar dev ...
.Nm
.Op Ar global options
.Cm list
.Ar dir
.Nm
.Op Ar global options
.Cm dump
.Ar dir candidate
.Op Ar dev ...
.Fl o Ar out.img
.Op Ar options
.Nm
.Op Ar global options
.Cm roots
.Fl o Ar dir
.Op Ar options
.Op Ar dev ...
.Nm
.Op Ar global options
.Cm zeropoint
.Op Ar options
.Op Ar dev ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
When the transaction groups that named a volume have been overwritten,
its blocks may still be on the members, addressed by nothing.
.Nm
scans the raw space of the members for the metadata of such volumes,
ranks what it finds into candidates in a workspace directory, and
extracts a candidate block by block, every block verified by its own
checksum.
.Cm roots
does the same for the pool's object set when no uberblock survives,
and
.Cm zeropoint
finds where a member's vdev begins from the pointers alone.
Nothing is written to the members.
.Pp
The global options and the pool member options are those of
.Xr zvolrescue 1 :
.Fl f , Fl -format Ar text | json ,
.Fl v , Fl -verbose ,
.Fl q , Fl -quiet ,
.Fl -evidence-log ,
.Fl -hash-inputs ,
.Fl -no-color ,
.Fl -debug ,
.Fl -debug-log ,
and
.Fl -image ,
.Fl -hints ,
.Fl -search-order ,
.Fl -pool-guid ,
.Fl -assume-member ,
.Fl -ignore-unknown-features ,
.Fl -map ,
.Fl -device-may-fail ,
.Fl -surface-scan-on-device .
.Sh COMMANDS
.Ss scan
.Nm Cm scan
.Fl o Ar dir
.Op Ar options
.Op Ar dev ...
.Pp
Scan the members and record what could be a volume.
A profile of the volume being looked for narrows the search; without
one every recognised dnode is a candidate.
The leaves of a mirror are copies of one another: a dnode found at the
same place on two of them is one candidate, and
.Pa candidates.json
counts the copies it left out
.Pq Li mirror_duplicates .
A scan that finds no candidate exits 3.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o , Fl -output Ar dir
The carve workspace, where candidates and the scan's state go.
The directory and both files in it are refused, with exit 5, if they
are anything the run reads or share bytes with it: a member under any
name, a map, the hints, the profile, a device a member is on, or the
path a log goes to.
.It Fl -volblocksize Ar bytes
Data block size of the volume being looked for.
.It Fl -levels Ar n
Tree depth of the volume being looked for.
.It Fl -txg Ar from Ns .. Ns Ar to
Birth transaction group window.
.It Fl -size Ar min Ns .. Ns Ar max
Estimated size range in bytes.
.It Fl -dnode-type Ar type
Object type to look for:
.Ql zvol
(the default, unless the profile names one),
.Ql file ,
a numeric dnode type, or
.Ql any .
Given here, it wins over the profile's.
.It Fl -profile Ar file
Read the profile from a JSON file; command-line fields win.
.It Fl -like Ar dataset
Take the block size, levels and size from a dataset that still exists
in the pool: in most incidents the lost volume was made like its
neighbours.
.It Fl -strict-profile
Treat profile fields as hard filters, not as hints.
.It Fl -range Ar start Ns - Ns Ar end
Only this byte range of each member; a
.Ar start
between two 512-byte slots is taken down to the slot it is in, and a
dnode that starts before
.Ar end
is read whole.
A scan done in parts, each resumed to the end of a longer range, is
the scan done in one, wherever the parts end.
A range that starts past the end of every member is a usage error.
.It Fl -resume
Continue a scan that was interrupted; a SIGINT or SIGTERM ends a scan
with its state written (exit status 6), and the candidates whose walk
the signal cut short are walked by the resume.
The workspace must be from a scan of the same pool and members, with
the same profile; another is refused (exit 1), and so is a state or an
index there that cannot be read.
With no state at all the scan starts from the beginning, and says so.
It goes on with the range the scan was started with; a
.Fl -range
given again may end elsewhere but must start where that one did.
A scan interrupted and resumed ends with the index one pass would have
written.
.It Fl -full-assess
Read every block a candidate claims when ranking it, rather than a
sample.
Slow, and rarely worth it before a
.Cm dump .
.It Fl -max-hits Ar n
Stop each member's scan once it has this many hits; default 100000.
The first
.Ar n
are listed, the scan says where it got to and exits with status 6, and
.Fl -resume
goes on from the first hit not listed, with the same limit or another.
.It Fl -sample Ar n
Report what the first
.Ar n
hits look like instead of filtering, so a profile can be picked from
the disk.
A sample that reached its size exits with status 0.
A limit of 0 keeps nothing and is a usage error, as is
.Fl -max-roots
0.
.It Fl -compressed Ar list
Compressions to try at each allocation-aligned offset:
.Ql lz4,lzjb,gzip,zstd
(the default), a subset, or
.Ql none
for the plaintext pass alone.
Metadata is lz4 on any modern pool and lzjb on one made before the
.Ql lz4_compress
feature; each one costs a pass, and dropping the wrong one finds
nothing.
The offsets are counted from where the vdev starts; on a member no
label, uberblock or
.Fl -hints
places, that start is found from its block pointers, as
.Cm zeropoint
finds it, said, and recorded in the index
.Pq Li starts_by_pointers .
Reading a candidate from such a member needs the same start in
.Fl -hints .
.El
.Ss list
.Nm Cm list
.Ar dir
.Pp
Show the candidates a previous scan found, ranked, with the id each
one is extracted by.
.Ss dump
.Nm Cm dump
.Ar dir candidate
.Op Ar dev ...
.Fl o Ar out.img
.Op Ar options
.Pp
Extract one candidate, verifying every block by its checksum.
The members must be those of the pool the scan read; another pool's
are refused (exit 1).
The result has the shape of
.Nm zvolrescue Cm dump Ns 's ,
a
.Li volumes
list with the ranges that could not be read, so that
.Xr zvolreport 1
reports the extraction.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o , Fl -output Ar out.img
Write the image here.
Refused, with exit 5, as the workspace of
.Cm scan
is, and also when it is the workspace's own index or a member the
index names, whatever names the members are given by now.
A pipe, or the run's own standard output or standard error, is refused
with exit 1.
.It Fl -strict
Stop at the first block that cannot be read.
.It Fl -size Ar bytes
Extract this many bytes instead of what the dnode implies.
.It Fl -hash Ar list
Also hash the image with
.Ql md5
and/or
.Ql sha1 ,
comma-separated; SHA-256 is always taken.
.It Fl -resume
Continue an interrupted extraction of the same candidate; the state is
kept in
.Pa out.img.resume.json
while a run is in progress.
The candidate is known there by a hash of its dnode, not only by its
id, which is no more than its place in one scan's list: the state of
another scan's candidate of the same id is not taken up.
As with
.Xr zvolrescue 1 ,
the ranges an interrupted run lost are reported by the run that
finishes the image, an output that refuses a block ends the run there
as a signal does (exit status 6), and the state of an image written to
a device is kept in the current directory under the device's path.
.El
.Ss roots
.Nm Cm roots
.Fl o Ar dir
.Op Ar options
.Op Ar dev ...
.Pp
Find the pool's object set when no uberblock survives: the members are
scanned for object set headers, which are ranked into
.Ar dir .
The same header on two sides of a mirror is listed once, with the
other members it is on
.Pq Li also_on .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o , Fl -output Ar dir
Carve workspace, where the ranked headers are written; refused as the
workspace of
.Cm scan
is.
.It Fl -range Ar start Ns - Ns Ar end
Only this byte range of each member.
.It Fl -compressed Ar list
Compressions to try, as in
.Cm scan .
.It Fl -max-roots Ar n
Keep at most this many headers after ranking; default 32.
.El
.Ss zeropoint
.Nm Cm zeropoint
.Op Ar options
.Op Ar dev ...
.Pp
Where the vdev begins, from the pointers alone: the base at which the
block pointers found on a member agree with the checksums of what they
point at.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o , Fl -output Ar dir
Also write
.Pa zeropoint.json
here; refused as the workspace of
.Cm scan
is.
.It Fl -window Ar start Ns - Ns Ar end
Consider only bases in this byte range; default the first 64 MiB,
where a partition begins.
.It Fl -compressed Ar list
Compressions to try, as in
.Cm scan .
.It Fl -max-hits Ar n
Collect probes from at most this many dnodes; default 4096.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa dir/state.json
The state of a scan, for
.Fl -resume .
No run writes a log over it, or over
.Pa dir/candidates.json .
.It Pa out.img.resume.json
The state of an interrupted
.Cm dump ;
for a device,
.Pa dev_da1.resume.json
in the current directory.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
As in
.Xr zvolrescue 1 :
0 success, 1 usage, 2 evidence unreadable, 3 nothing found (a scan
with no candidate) or the candidate unreadable, 4 a partial image or a scan that could not read
every byte of the range (what lay in the bytes it could not read was not
searched, and
.Pa candidates.json
says how many), 5 refused, 6 interrupted, stopped at
.Fl -max-hits ,
or stopped by an output that refused the image, with the state written, 7 the medium refused a read.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A volume made like its surviving neighbour, found and extracted from
images of two mirror members:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
zvolcarve scan --like pool/vm/disk1 -o carve \e
    --image ada0.img --image ada1.img
zvolcarve list carve
zvolcarve dump carve c0003 --image ada0.img --image ada1.img -o disk0.img
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr zvolfiles 1 ,
.Xr zvolreport 1 ,
.Xr zvolrescue 1 ,
.Xr zvoltimeline 1
